UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Predicting and questioning', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Make predictions from evidence and ask questions that deepen understanding.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Predict what happens after \"The dog scratched at the back door and whined\".\nCOMPARE: Perhaps it wants to go outside. Scratching at the door and whining are clues; this is not certain.\n2. Ask one question after \"Leah stared at the empty nest\".\nCOMPARE: \"Where did the birds go?\" or \"How does Leah feel?\" are useful questions because the text raises uncertainty.\n3. Your prediction is a lost dog. New line: \"Its owner whistled and it came running.\" Update your idea.\nCOMPARE: The dog may not be lost; it recognises its owner and responds to the whistle.\n4. Identify fact and prediction: \"The path was wet, so I think someone used the hose.\"\nCOMPARE: Fact: path was wet. Prediction/inference: someone used the hose; rain or another cause is also possible.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nRead: \"The library lights flickered. Ella heard a click from the corridor. She shut her book and walked towards the desk.\" Write two predictions with different clues, three useful questions and an updated prediction after adding your own plausible next sentence. Label facts versus possible explanations.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Make predictions from evidence and ask questions that deepen understanding.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read: \"The library lights flickered. Ella heard a click from the corridor. She shut her book and walked towards the desk.\" Write two predictions with different clues, three useful questions and an updated prediction afte Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using prediction on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction.. Correction: Perhaps it wants to go outside.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating prediction on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with prediction, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about prediction with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for prediction. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for prediction. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Perhaps it wants to go outside","A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction"],"question":"Ask one question after \"Leah stared at the empty nest\". Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for prediction; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction","Make predictions from evidence and ask questions that deepen understanding","Perhaps it wants to go outside","Careful use of question and point to the words or event with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with prediction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named question and point to the words or event, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name question and point to the words or event only, without linking it to prediction","Apply prediction to new material and name the question and point to the words or event used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction"],"question":"Which response best applies prediction with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries prediction. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction","Point to exact words in the sentence that show prediction and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses prediction from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name question and point to the words or event without linking it to any words in the text","Perhaps it wants to go outside","A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about prediction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse question and point to the words or event?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for prediction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about prediction"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of prediction on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about prediction with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating prediction on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using prediction on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for prediction. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with prediction, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction.. Correction: Perhaps it wants to go outside.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w03-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise prediction.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Make predictions from evidence and ask questions that deepen understanding."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: \"Where did the birds go?\" or \"How does Leah feel?\" are useful questions because the text raises uncertainty."],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: Text: \"Clouds darkened and Mara packed her picnic blanket.\" Prediction: Mara expects rain. Evidence: dark clouds and packing. I may be wrong"],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Do not cling to your first prediction after contrary evidence appears."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Make predictions from evidence and ask questions that deepen understanding."],"teach0":["Focus on predict, do not simply guess.","Hold this teaching point: A prediction is a reasonable idea about what might happen next based on clues and what you already know. Explain the clu","Worked comparison: Perhaps it wants to go outside. Scratching at the door and whining are clues; this is not certain."],"teach1":["Focus on ask questions while reading.","Hold this teaching point: Questions about who, why, how and what might happen help us monitor meaning. Some answers appear directly; others need i","Worked comparison: \"Where did the birds go?\" or \"How does Leah feel?\" are useful questions because the text raises uncertainty."],"teach2":["Focus on update a prediction with new evidence.","Hold this teaching point: A good reader changes a prediction when new details make it less likely. Revising is thoughtful, not failure.","Worked comparison: The dog may not be lost; it recognises its owner and responds to the whistle."],"teach3":["Focus on distinguish fact, clue and possibility.","Hold this teaching point: A fact is stated; a clue is a detail we use; a prediction is what may occur. Keep them separate when explaining.","Worked comparison: Fact: path was wet. Prediction/inference: someone used the hose; rain or another cause is also possible."],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Read: \"The library lights flickered. Ella heard a click from the corridor. She shut her book and walked towards the desk.\" Write two predictions with different "]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use prediction with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify question and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise clue independently on a new prompt.","I can notice revise needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying prediction independently"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about prediction with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nText: \"Clouds darkened and Mara packed her picnic blanket.\" Prediction: Mara expects rain. Evidence: dark clouds and packing. I may be wrong, but I have a reason.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nPredict what happens after \"The dog scratched at the back door and whined\".\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nPerhaps it wants to go outside. Scratching at the door and whining are clues; this is not certain.","explain":"A prediction is a reasonable idea about what might happen next based on clues and what you already know. Explain the clue.\n\nA prediction is a reasonable idea about what might happen next based on clues and what you already know. Explain the clue. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nA wild guess with no textual reason is not a supported prediction. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Predict, do not simply guess","visual":[{"alt":"Dark clouds used as a clue for the prediction that Mara packed for rain.","caption":"A prediction needs a clue from the text, not a wild guess.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Clue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Clouds darkened</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Supported prediction</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Mara packed because rain may come</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.","who":"juno"},{"text":"So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Text: \"Clouds darkened and Mara packed her picnic blanket.\" Prediction: Mara expects rain. Evidence: dark clouds and pac Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.\nPip: So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Text: \"Clouds darkened and Mara packed her picnic blanket.\" Prediction: Mara expects rain. Evidence: dark clouds and pac Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying question independently","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about prediction with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nReading \"Sam left the gate open\", I ask \"Who noticed?\" and \"Could an animal escape?\" The first may be answered later; the second is a possibility worth checking.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nAsk one question after \"Leah stared at the empty nest\".\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"Where did the birds go?\" or \"How does Leah feel?\" are useful questions because the text raises uncertainty.","explain":"Questions about who, why, how and what might happen help us monitor meaning. Some answers appear directly; others need inference.\n\nQuestions about who, why, how and what might happen help us monitor meaning. Some answers appear directly; others need inference. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nAn interesting question is not necessarily answered by the available text. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Ask questions while reading","visual":[{"alt":"A first storm prediction revised when smoke from a bushfire explains the dark sky.","caption":"New evidence can change a prediction; revising is thoughtful, not failure.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dark sky means storm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Updated idea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">smoke from a bushfire</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying clue independently"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about prediction with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nAt first I predict a storm because the sky is dark. Then the story says a bushfire is spreading smoke. I update: dark sky came from smoke, not rain.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nYour prediction is a lost dog. New line: \"Its owner whistled and it came running.\" Update your idea.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nThe dog may not be lost; it recognises its owner and responds to the whistle.","explain":"A good reader changes a prediction when new details make it less likely. Revising is thoughtful, not failure.\n\nA good reader changes a prediction when new details make it less likely. Revising is thoughtful, not failure. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not cling to your first prediction after contrary evidence appears. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Update a prediction with new evidence"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying revise independently"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about prediction with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The torch flickered\" is a stated fact. It may be low on batteries is an inference or prediction. \"It will certainly stop\" goes beyond what is known.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nIdentify fact and prediction: \"The path was wet, so I think someone used the hose.\"\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nFact: path was wet. Prediction/inference: someone used the hose; rain or another cause is also possible.","explain":"A fact is stated; a clue is a detail we use; a prediction is what may occur. Keep them separate when explaining.\n\nA fact is stated; a clue is a detail we use; a prediction is what may occur. Keep them separate when explaining. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not claim that your idea happened simply because it is plausible. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Distinguish fact, clue and possibility"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Predicting and questioning","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":3,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — Text: \"Clouds darkened and Mara packed her picnic blanket.\" Prediction: Mara expects rain. Evidence: dark clouds and packing. I may be wrong, but I have a reason.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. Reading \"Sam left the gate open\", I ask \"Who noticed?\" and \"Could an animal escape?\" The first may be answered later; the second is a possibility worth checking.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Dark clouds used as a clue for the prediction that Mara packed for rain.","caption":"A prediction needs a clue from the text, not a wild guess.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Clue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Clouds darkened</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Supported prediction</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Mara packed because rain may come</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A first storm prediction revised when smoke from a bushfire explains the dark sky.","caption":"New evidence can change a prediction; revising is thoughtful, not failure.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dark sky means storm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Updated idea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">smoke from a bushfire</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y3-w03-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
